toolCallRounds.response:
No — strtok_r (and strtok) skip empty fields, so your current loop will mis-align when the buffer contains consecutive delimiters (empty fields). That explains why an empty sender ("") causes the filename to shift into the wrong field.

Fix: split while preserving empty tokens. Two simple options:

- Use strsep (returns empty token for "||").
- Or use a manual scan with strchr.

Example replacement using strsep (short and clear). This change goes into common.c, replacing deserialize_message's token loop.

````c
// filepath: /home/shrawani/course-project-shellshocked/common.c
// ...existing code...
void deserialize_message(char *buffer, Message *msg) {
    init_message(msg);
    char *s = buffer;
    char *token;
    int field = 0;

    while ((token = strsep(&s, "|")) != NULL && field < 10) {
        switch(field) {
            case 0: if (token[0] != '\0') msg->type = atoi(token); break;
            case 1: if (token[0] != '\0') msg->status = atoi(token); break;
            case 2: strncpy(msg->sender, token, MAX_USERNAME-1); break;
            case 3: strncpy(msg->filename, token, MAX_FILENAME-1); break;
            case 4: if (token[0] != '\0') msg->sentence_index = atoi(token); break;
            case 5: if (token[0] != '\0') msg->word_index = atoi(token); break;
            case 6: if (token[0] != '\0') msg->ss_id = atoi(token); break;
            case 7: if (token[0] != '\0') msg->access = atoi(token); break;
            case 8: strncpy(msg->target_user, token, MAX_USERNAME-1); break;
            case 9: strncpy(msg->data, token, MAX_BUFFER-1); break;
        }
        field++;
    }
}
// ...existing code...
````

Notes
- strsep is available on Linux/POSIX; it preserves empty tokens so the field indices remain correct.
- The numeric fields check token[0] so existing default values from init_message (e.g., -1) are preserved when the token is empty.
- Alternatively, use a manual strchr-based parser if you prefer not to use strsep.

