Short answer
- const char* promises the function will not modify the string data. It documents intent, prevents accidental writes (compiler will warn/error if you try), and lets you pass string literals or const data without casts.

Why it matters
- Safety: protects callers — you can't accidentally change a string literal or a const buffer.
- Correctness: callers with const data (or string literals) can call the function without casting away const.
- Self-documenting: shows readers the function only reads the string.
- No runtime cost: it's a compile-time constraint only.

Practical notes
- You can pass a char* to a function expecting const char* (implicit), but not vice‑versa.
- If the function must modify the string, keep char* or make an internal copy and modify that.
- Update both the prototype and definition to const char* so declarations match.

