What's happening
- recv_message(...) failed (returned < 0) so your code goes into the error branch and then prints msg.status.
- But when recv fails the msg struct is left as whatever was on the stack (or from a previous iteration). That's why you see 200 — it's a stale value, not a valid response from the peer.
- Common causes of recv returning negative here: socket timeout (SO_RCVTIMEO → errno == EAGAIN/EWOULDBLOCK), interrupted syscall (EINTR), or real socket error/peer closed connection. recv_message typically does not zero msg on error.

Fixes
- Do not access msg when recv_message fails. Check the return value / errno and handle timeouts, disconnects and errors explicitly.
- Optionally call init_message(&msg) or memset(&msg,0,sizeof(msg)) before recv to avoid stale data if you ever inspect msg after errors (but better to not inspect it at all).

